adjudication.rationale:
In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the only request authentication is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

This comparison can trivially succeed when the secret is not configured:
- If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is unset, `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is `undefined`. If the header is also absent, `req.headers[...]` is `undefined`, so `undefined !== undefined` is false and the request is accepted (fail-open).
- The diff also adds `.env.example` with `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""`. If a self-hoster copies that and leaves it empty, a caller can send the configured header with an empty value and pass the check (`"" !== ""` is false).

No other auth (method checks, signature/HMAC, etc.) is present in this route, so the finding describes a real security defect introduced by the diff.

In the new endpoint `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook secret is validated via a direct inequality check:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is a non-constant-time comparison of a shared secret, which is a known class of security weakness (timing side-channel) compared to using `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on normalized `Buffer`s. The handler also introduces no throttling/rate limiting in this diff, so repeated probing is not mitigated within this code path. The finding is therefore grounded in and verifiable from the diff.
